Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> Charlie schrieb:
>
>> For example, I have a Samurai skilled in long sword and basic in
>> dagger.
>> Should I hone up either of those skills before going developing two
>> weaponing? And why?
>
> The minimum skill of each weapon and of your two-weapon proficiency
> is effective. So enhance both, your weapon skills and two-weaponing
> skill; first enhance the individual weapon's proficiency exercising
> single-weapon combat, then exercise your two-weaponing proficiency.
> If your character can only get Basic in any of the skills it's an
> inferior weapon choice; pick those (class appropriate) weapons whose
> proficiencies you can maximize. It's also a waste to advance skill
> slots to Expert[*] if you intend two-weaponing but can't get beyond
> Skilled with either of the used weapons.

As already mentioned it's the lower of the main wield skill and the two
weapon skill.  The alternate wield weapon is not counted for hit
percentage (it counts for damage if you hit).  So practice up the main
wield, the practice up the two weapon back and forth until both are at
least Skilled.  ACtually I tend to exercise the long sword skill to
Skilled well before I even start using #twoweapon for the following
reasons -

>> I have noticed that two weaponing is very weak in the very early game.

Certainly.  It costs a samurai 2 skill slots to get to Skilled in long
sword and 3 skill slots to get to Skilled in two weapon.  That's XP=6
before it could possibly happen.  And it takes a *ton* of damage hits
before it allows you that many enhancements.  Using #twoweapon is a bad
idea in the early game because of the lost hits and damamges.  It's the
mid-game before it starts becoming powerfull.

> [*] Of course it still makes sense to get, say, daggers to Expert
> even if you can only to Skilled with two-weaponing, because your
> daggers skill will also be very useful when using them as missiles.

That and any class who can get to Expert in dagger should do so as early
as possible because of the multifire.  It costs a samurai 6 slots to get
to Expert in dagger and the benefit of doing that is far larger than the
benefit of getting to Skilled in #twoweapon.  Maybe even better than
going to Skilled in long sword.  So by the time my samurais can get to
Skilled in #twoweapon (3) they are Expert in dagger (6) plus Skilled in
long sword (2) or XP=11.  That's approaching the mid-game.

Beyond that samurai can also become Expert in bow and with a yumi firing
ya they get an even higher firing rate.  It is very nice to be able to
face the drawbridge with a lot of arrows in your quiver and wipe out the
hoards as they walk the plank.

Starting at XP=12 it starts being worth enhancing bow towards Expert
(5) sooner than long sword towards Expert. Dagger (6) plus bow (5)
plus long sword (5) to Expert plus #twoweapon to Skilled (3) you might
end up with those after the Quest.


